====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[waste#Verb|waste]] away; to [[fade#Verb|fade]], to [[wither]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|now|rare}} To [[daunt]] or [[frighten]] (someone). {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Garnier Kyd Cornelia|act=I|sig=B|verso=1|page=20|passage=Death dvvels vvithin vs, and if gentle Peace / Diſcend not ſoone, our ſorrovves to ſurceaſe, / ''Latium'' (alreadie '''quaild''') vvill be deſtroyd.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Antony and Cleopatra|act=V|scene=ii|page=365|column=2|passage=But when he meant to '''quaile''' and shake the Orbe, / He was as ratling Thunder.}}
#* '''1978''', {{w|Lawrence Durrell}}, ''[[w:Livia (novel)|Livia: or, Buried Alive: A Novel]]'', London; Boston, Mass.: {{w|Faber and Faber}}, <small>{{ISBN|978-0-571-11297-5}}</small>; republished in ''{{w|The Avignon Quintet}}'', London: Faber, published 1992, <small>{{ISBN|978-0-571-16328-1}}</small>, page 358:
#*: To tell the truth the prospect rather '''quailed''' him&nbsp;– wandering about in the gloomy corridors of a nunnery.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[lose heart]] or [[courage]]; to be [[daunted#Adjective|daunted]] or [[fearful]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Thackeray Vanity Fair|chapter=A Quarrel about an Heiress|page=183|passage=Though George had stopped in his sentence, yet, his blood being up, he was not to be cowed by all the generations of Osborne; rallying instantly, he replied to the bullying look of his father, with another so indicative of resolution and defiance, that the elder man '''quailed''' in his turn, and looked away.}}
#* {{RQ:Stevenson Jekyll and Hyde|chapter=The Carew Murder Case|page=39|passage=Mr. Utterson had already '''quailed''' at the name of Hyde; but when the stick was laid before him, he could doubt no longer: broken and battered as it was, he recognized it for one that he had himself presented many years before to Henry Jekyll.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Seymour S. Tibbals|title=The Puritans or The Captain of Plymouth: A Comic Opera in Three Acts|location=[Franklin, Oh.]|publisher=Seymour S. Tibbals|year=1904|page=13|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=xthZAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA59|section=act II, scene i|oclc=20218813|passage=Stouter hearts than a woman's have '''quailed''' in this terrible winter. Yours is tender and trusting, and needs a stronger one to lean on; so I have come to you now, with an offer of marriage.}}
#* {{RQ:Orwell Nineteen Eighty-Four|chapter=2|page=27|passage=The sun had shifted round, and the myriad windows of the Ministry of Truth, with the light no longer shining on them, looked grim as the loopholes of a fortress. His heart '''quailed''' before the enormous pyramidal shape. It was too strong, it could not be stormed.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Obituary: Antonin Scalia: Always right|url=http://www.economist.com/news/obituary/21693161-originalist-chief-devout-and-colourful-end-was-79-obituary-antonin-scalia|magazine=w:The Economist|date=20 February 2016|passage=His colleagues '''quailed''' when, in 1986, he first sat on the court as a brash 50-year-old whose experience had been mostly as a combative government lawyer: a justice who, in that sanctum of columns and deep judicial silence, was suddenly firing questions like grapeshot.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of courage, [[faith]], etc.: to [[slacken]], to [[give way]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=I|canto=VIII|stanza=14|page=108|passage=Therewith his ſturdie corage ſoone was '''quayd''', / And all his ſences were with ſuddein dread diſmayd.}}
#* {{RQ:Trollope He Knew|chapter=Hard Words|page=77|passage="Sir, if you think your name is shamed by me, we had better part," said Mrs. Trevelyan, rising from her chair, and confronting him with a look before which his own almost '''quailed'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Book of the Saints of the Ethiopian Church[:] A translation of the Ethiopic Synaxarium {{...}}|passage=And he commanded his soldiers {{...}} to frighten them with fierce swords, but the hearts of the holy men did not '''quail''', and they were unable to alter their words.|year=1928|page=220|translator=w:E. A. Wallis Budge|pageurl=https://www.google.com.au/books/edition/The_Book_of_the_Saints_of_the_Ethiopian/-0PZAAAAMAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&pg=PA220|volume=1|publisher=Cambridge University Press|location=London}}

