===Etymology 2===
{{wikipedia}}
[[File:Caille des blés - Coturnix coturnix.JPG|thumb|a [[common quail|common '''quail''']] ({{taxfmt|Coturnix coturnix|species}})]]
From {{inh|en|enm|quayle}}, {{m|enm|quaile}}, {{m|enm|quaille}}, from {{der|en|xno|quaille}}, from {{der|en|LL.|quaccola|t=quail}}.

{{sense|prostitute}} So called because the quail was thought to be a very [[amorous]] bird.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~|s|quail}}

# {{senseid|en|Q6072584}}Any of various small [[game bird]]s of the genera {{taxfmt|Coturnix|genus}}, {{taxfmt|Anurophasis|genus}} or {{taxfmt|Perdicula|genus}} in the [[Old World]] family {{taxfmt|Phasianidae|family}} or of the [[New World]] family {{taxfmt|Odontophoridae|family}}.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1954|journal=Wildlife Review|issue=75-83| page=44| passage='''Quail''' require little water, so there is no point to putting in a [[guzzler]] if there is any permanent water within travel range.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The meat from this bird eaten as food.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[prostitute]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:William Shakespeare|title=[[w:Troilus and Cressida|The Famous Historie of Troylus and Cresseid. Excellently Expressing the Beginning of their Loues, with the Conceited Wooing of Pandarus, Prince of Licia]]|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=XLs2AQAAMAAJ| location=London| publisher=Imprinted by [[w:George Eld|G[eorge] Eld]] for R[ichard] Bonian and H[enry] Walley, and are to be sold at the spred Eagle in [[w:St Paul's Cathedral|Paules Church-yeard]], ouer against the great North doore| year=c. 1602| year_published=1609| oclc=606515252| section=act V, scene 1| passage=Her's ''Agamemnon'', an honeſt fellow inough and one that loues '''quailes''', but hee has not ſo much braine as eare-wax, {{...}}}}

